 {"evidence_run_id": "a9b1feeb", "status": "complete", "id": "AUG-150", "title": "Obligation gate is still bypassable: a verification block with method=\"test\" and a path but no type=\"test\" is never checked", "detail": {"fingerprint": "0407d2ec118088b39b8846b8c9cf2361065fd3758650f5261cfe30725282e865", "evidence": [{"run_id": "a9b1feeb", "ts": "2026-11-11T02:02:35.42686+00:00", "tree_fp": "fdb2c8b3dbb3ca62a4f7764e24ad5fb340b94c800f77db1cddacc305f4a8442f", "artifacts": [".gitreins/history/2026-11-11/a9b1feeb/verdict.json"]}]}, "priority": "P1", "source": null, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-05 on the merged wave tree (2815262 + corpus). Reproduce: take the shipped specs/verification.xml and change one verification block's path to tests/does-not-exist.py, leaving method=\"test\" runner=\"pytest\" selector=... intact -> `auger spec check specs` returns {\"status\": \"valid\"} and `auger spec validate specs` rc 0. Add type=\"test\" to the same block -> the same corpus is refused: 'stale-test-ref: verification.xml: SPEC-OBL-CONSUMER-V1: tests/does-not-exist.py'. So the obligation checks (tracked path, selector, fixture, migration) are keyed on the presence of the `type` attribute, exactly the shape AUG-128 was filed to remove; AUG-128 fixed `method` for the block KIND check but not for obligation validation. Impact: the newly authored corpus (AUG-136) used method+path+selector without type, so EVERY test citation in specs/ was unverified by the tool — the corpus was green while a fabricated path would also be green. Coordinator worked around it by writing type=\"test\" on the 7 citation blocks; the product hole remains and the next author will hit it.", "review_notes": null, "ts": null, "acceptance_criteria": "PASS: obligation validation keys on `type=\"test\"` OR `method=\"test\"` (the AUG-128 rule), proven by a fixture pair that differs only in which attribute carries the word test: both are refused when their path does not exist, naming document + block + resolved p